# Compiled binaries. Every one is written to the repo root — `make build`
# produces ./opx, `make cross` produces ./opx-darwin-{arm64,amd64} — so these
# are anchored with a leading slash and cannot swallow a nested path.
#
# The pattern is a glob rather than the four exact names it replaces: those
# covered what the Makefile happens to build today, so any other binary left
# at the root (a comparison build, a bisect artifact, an ad-hoc `go build -o`)
# was untracked-but-not-ignored and went straight into the next `git add -A`.
# That is not hypothetical — it put a 9.3 MB binary into #27.
#
# `opx-*` rather than `opx*`: the latter would also ignore a source file or
# directory whose name starts with opx. Go source uses underscores, so
# `opx_test.go` and `opxutil/` stay visible while `opx-prefix` does not.
/opx
/opx-*

# Test binaries from `go test -c`
*.test

# Go workspace files
go.work
go.work.sum

